Requested by Max 2026-08-31, off the back of B094: "we need some test fixture
plugins that use the angelscript sorting functions that take a function handle
and make sure that we match those and it all works as expected and we can
detect when a function handle that is provided matches or not."

## Verified broken (2026-08-31, live binary, master post-B071/B089)

Fixture, `check` reports ONE diagnostic and it is an unrelated unused-function
warning. Every bad callback is silent:

```angelscript
class Item { int v; }
bool LessInt(const int &in a, const int &in b) { return a < b; }
bool LessItem(const Item@ &in a, const Item@ &in b) { return a.v < b.v; }
bool WrongArity(const int &in a) { return a < 0; }
bool WrongTypes(const string &in a, const string &in b) { return a < b; }
void WrongReturn(const int &in a, const int &in b) { }

void Use() {
    int[] xs = {3,1,2};
    xs.Sort(LessInt);      // good      -> silent (correct)
    xs.Sort(WrongArity);   // BAD arity -> SILENT
    xs.Sort(WrongTypes);   // BAD types -> SILENT
    xs.Sort(WrongReturn);  // BAD ret   -> SILENT
    xs.Sort(LessItem);     // BAD elem  -> SILENT
    array<Item@> items;
    items.Sort(LessItem);  // good      -> silent (correct)
    items.Sort(LessInt);   // BAD elem  -> SILENT
}
```

## Why this is NOT just B089 again

B089 checks a funcdef-typed parameter of a WORKSPACE function at a workspace
call site. That path is live and gated. This is the ENGINE side, and it has a
shape nothing else in the typedb has:

    void Sort(T[]::less&in, uint startAt = 0, uint count = uint(-1))
    void SortNonConst(T[]::less_nonconst&in, uint startAt = 0, uint count = uint(-1))

`T[]::less` is a TEMPLATE-MEMBER funcdef. Its parameter types are the array's
element type, so the expected signature is not fixed -- it must be computed per
call site by substituting T:

  * `int[]`      -> `bool(const int&in, const int&in)`
  * `Item@[]`    -> `bool(const Item@&in, const Item@&in)`

No other funcdef slot in the codebase needs substitution. Every existing
funcdef check compares against a fixed declared signature.

## The typedb does not declare `less` at all

Measured against `tests/fixtures/typedb/OpenplanetCore.json`:

| typeid | what | status |
|---|---|---|
| 67108888 | `less` funcdef (referenced by `Sort`'s first arg) | **DANGLING -- referenced, never declared** |
| 67108889 | `less_nonconst` funcdef | DECLARED: `bool less_nonconst(T&in a, T&in b)` |
| 67112305 | `T` template parameter type | **DANGLING -- referenced, never declared** |

`funcdefs` contains exactly: CoroutineFunc, CoroutineFuncUserdata,
CoroutineFuncUserdataBool, CoroutineFuncUserdataDouble,
CoroutineFuncUserdataInt64, CoroutineFuncUserdataString,
CoroutineFuncUserdataUint64, InputTextCallback, NodTreeMemberCallback,
ProcIntercept, ProcInterceptEx, less_nonconst. No `less`.

So the const form has no declaration to check against and its signature must be
SYNTHESISED. `bool less(const T&in a, const T&in b)` is the obvious reading --
by analogy with the declared `less_nonconst` and with the AngelScript array
add-on -- but it is a GUESS until probed. **Probe the game before relying on
it.** In particular settle whether the engine accepts a `bool(T&in, T&in)`
(non-const) callback at the const `Sort` slot, and whether it accepts a
`const T&in` callback at the `SortNonConst` slot. Those two answers decide
whether the two slots need separate rules or one.

## Why nobody noticed, and why the fleet sweep will not notice either

**Zero call sites across the whole fixture corpus.** `grep -rn '\.Sort(' tests/fixtures/
--include='*.as'` excluding no-arg `.Sort()` returns 0 matches, and so does
`.SortNonConst(`. Every plugin that sorts either uses `SortAsc`/`SortDesc` or
hand-rolls a quicksort taking its own workspace funcdef -- which is the
tm-better-totd `totdsQuicksort(allTotds, Less_OldFirst)` shape, i.e. the B089
path, not this one.

This matters for the gate. A fleet sweep over 151 plugins is the standard
"did we break anything" signal here, and for this rule it is structurally
blind: zero delta is guaranteed regardless of whether the code works. Do not
report a sweep as evidence for this ticket. A positive control on a real
plugin is required, and it will have to be an INJECTED `.Sort(cb)` call site,
since no shipped plugin provides one.

## Work

1. Probe the engine first (see above). Record verdicts verbatim in the ticket.
   Per B068, probe each fixture as its OWN compilation unit -- never batched.
   REJECT-direction verdicts are the trustworthy ones on this machine.
2. Resolve `T[]::less` / `T[]::less_nonconst` at an array method call site to a
   concrete signature by substituting the receiver's element type.
3. Check the argument against it: arity, parameter types, parameter MODES
   (`&in` vs `&out` vs `&inout` -- B027 already enforces exact modes for
   delegate construction, reuse that comparison rather than writing a second
   one), and return type.
4. Fixture plugins under `tests/fixtures/`, plugin-shaped (with `info.toml`),
   not just inline strings -- Max asked for fixture plugins specifically.

## Test matrix (generalise per repo CLAUDE.md)

Element types: `int`, `float`, `string`, `Item` (value class), `Item@`
(handle), `array<int>` (nested), an engine type (`CGameCtnChallenge@`), an
enum, a `dictionary@`.

Callback shapes, each x good/bad:
  * exact `bool(const T&in, const T&in)` -- accept
  * non-const `bool(T&in, T&in)` at `Sort` -- verdict per probe
  * const at `SortNonConst` -- verdict per probe
  * `&out` / `&inout` parameters -- reject
  * by-value `bool(T, T)` -- verdict per probe
  * wrong arity (1 arg, 3 args) -- reject
  * wrong element type (`int[]` given an `Item` comparer) -- reject
  * wrong return (`void`, `int`) -- reject
  * a class METHOD passed bare -- reject (see B091, this is the same wrapping
    rule the CoroutineFunc ticket covers)
  * a `funcdef` handle VARIABLE of the right shape -- accept
  * a null literal -- verdict per probe
  * the 3-arg form `xs.Sort(cb, 0, 5)` -- the extra `uint` args must not
    disturb the funcdef slot
  * `SortAsc()` / `SortDesc()` / `SortAsc(0,5)` stay silent (control)
  * the WORKSPACE quicksort shape (`totdsQuicksort(arr, Less_OldFirst)`) stays
    working -- that is B089's path and must not regress

Include a NEGATIVE control: a correct callback on every element type must stay
silent, or a rule that rejects everything scores as a pass.

